# KM Tracker — backup configuration (self-hosted "personalserver" box).
#
# Copy this file to `.env.backup` in the repo root and fill in values:
#     cp .env.backup.example .env.backup
#
# The real `.env.backup` is GITIGNORED and must NEVER be committed. Even so,
# there are no secrets here — the rclone Google Drive OAuth token lives only in
# rclone's own config (~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf), never in this file.
#
# Every var has a sane default in scripts/backup.sh; uncomment to override.

# Live SQLite DB on the host (bind-mounted into the container at /data).
# NB: this is WAL-mode and container-owned. Never restore by copying a snapshot
# over it — the stale -wal beside it gets replayed and you silently get the OLD
# data back (and the next checkpoint bakes it in). Follow DEPLOY.md → "Restore
# from a snapshot": stop the container, delete the -wal/-shm, copy, chown
# 10001:10001, start, then VERIFY.
# DB_PATH=/home/<user>/km-tracker/data/km_tracker.db

# The snapshot itself runs INSIDE the app container (as UID 10001), because the
# WAL-mode DB's -wal/-shm sidecars are container-owned and the online backup API
# must write them — a host-side backup fails "attempt to write a readonly
# database". These name the container and the DB path INSIDE it. The backup user
# must be able to run `docker` (in the `docker` group).
# BACKUP_CONTAINER=km-tracker-app-1
# CONTAINER_DB_PATH=/data/km_tracker.db

# Where on-box snapshots are written. Defaults OUTSIDE the container-owned
# data/ dir (UID 10001) so the host backup process can create/write it.
# LOCAL_BACKUP_DIR=/home/<user>/km-backups/snapshots

# Internal state (checksums + last-Drive-push timestamp). Also outside data/.
# STATE_DIR=/home/<user>/km-backups/state

# rclone remote + folder to push snapshots to. The remote ("gdrive") is created
# with `rclone config` (see DEPLOY.md); the folder is auto-created on first copy.
# REQUIRED for the Drive push — if unset, local snapshots still run but the
# Drive push is skipped with an error.
RCLONE_DEST=gdrive:km-tracker-backups

# Retention: keep the newest N snapshots locally and on Drive (the rolling
# "recent" ring buffer). Must be positive integers (>= 1).
# LOCAL_RETENTION=100
# DRIVE_RETENTION=50

# Daily long-tail tier: in addition to the rolling DRIVE_RETENTION snapshots,
# the script keeps at most ONE snapshot per UTC day in a `daily/` subfolder under
# RCLONE_DEST, retained for the newest N days. This defends against unnoticed
# logical corruption that would otherwise rotate out of the fast-moving recent
# ring buffer. Must be a positive integer (>= 1). Default 30 (~a month of days).
DAILY_RETENTION=30

# Minimum minutes between successful Drive pushes (the timer fires more often
# than this; the script throttles the off-box push to this cadence).
# DRIVE_PUSH_INTERVAL_MIN=15

# Safety valve. The script refuses a snapshot with ZERO rows in every table when
# the previous snapshot had data — that's what a /data remounted empty looks
# like (the container's entrypoint recreates a valid but empty DB on start), and
# backing it up would rotate every good copy out of the ring. Set to 1 only if
# the DB really was emptied on purpose and you want it backed up anyway.
# ALLOW_EMPTY_SNAPSHOT=0
